    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a lineup anchored by **Junior Caminero** (67.36) and **Christian Yelich** (64.16), the clear pillars of our offensive output. **Junior Caminero** benefits from a steady environment against Jos\u00e9 Soriano, while **Christian Yelich** draws a massive +13% platoon boost that compensates for facing the formidable Aaron Nola. **Dillon Dingler** (61.52) also emerges as a high-value play; his matchup against a left-handed arm combined with a favorable #4 lineup spot creates an ideal scenario for production. We are also leaning on **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (60.59), whose elite sprint speed is a priority given our focus on the stolen base category, and he maintains high utility here despite the daunting task of facing Trevor McDonald.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench today was between **Luke Raley** (52.18) and our current starters. **Luke Raley** was firmly in the mix, particularly with his +7% platoon advantage, but he was ultimately squeezed out by the efficiency of **Carson Benge** (52.34) and **Nelson Velazquez** (52.51), the latter of whom carries an impressive 0.482 xwOBA into his matchup against Prielipp. We are keeping a close eye on **Trea Turner** (56.84) and **Yordan Alvarez** (60.26), both of whom are projected to deliver strong counting stats despite facing tough lefties in Shane Drohan and Noah Cameron. \n\nRisk management remains paramount today, as nearly every lineup spot relies on \"Lineup Pending\" assumptions. **Casey Schmitt** (48.43) is an intriguing start\u2014his score is bolstered by a +11% platoon edge and a wind-out factor, but facing a pitcher like Ben Brown keeps his ceiling tempered. While **Caleb Durbin** (44.04) provided a viable floor, the Optimizer favored our current starters to preserve daily game caps, benching him primarily because of the prohibitive matchup against Jacob deGrom. We remain vigilant on weather and final lineup confirmations to ensure our high-efficiency stack, including **Colt Keith** (49.63) and **Kazuma Okamoto** (45.69), isn't disrupted by late-scratch scenarios.",
